<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glorious Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a>–<a href="/years/1689">1689</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Revolution of 1688</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Glorious Revolution of 1688–1689 overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James II of England</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and absolutism had alienated his subjects. William of Orange landed with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army in November 1688, James fled, and Parliament offered the crown to William III and Mary II with the Bill of Rights. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> the news triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> revolt of April 1689 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Edmund Andros</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution.jpg" alt="Glorious Revolution" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johan Herman Isings · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_of_Orange_III_and_his_Dutch_army_land_in_Brixham,_1688.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conflict between the Stuart kings and Parliament led to civil war, the execution of Charles I and a republic, and the <b>Glorious Revolution</b> of 1688 and the Bill of Rights of 1689 confirmed parliamentary supremacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Great Britain was a constitutional monarchy in which Parliament, made supreme by the <b>Revolution of 1688</b>, controlled taxation and the standing army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Revolution of 1688</b>–1689 the Scottish Parliament became more assertive, while economic weakness grew acute: the famines of the 1690s, known as the ill years, and the failure of the Darien scheme, a colony on the Isthmus of Panama in 1698–1700 that lost a large share of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Glorious Revolution</b> of 1688–1689 brought a Bill of Rights and an Act of Toleration for Protestant dissenters, and the lapse of the Licensing Act in 1695 ended censorship before publication.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles I of England ruled without Parliament from 1629 to 1640, but civil war, his execution in 1649 and the <b>Glorious Revolution</b> of 1688 ended moves towards absolute monarchy in England.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas Babington Macaulay’s History of England told the <b>Revolution of 1688</b> as a consensual and bloodless triumph of English liberty and limited monarchy, the classic Whig account.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
